      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "We estimated the risk of sudden cardiac death (SCD), from a spontaneous episode of ventricular arrhythmia (VT/VF), after a successful surgical myocardial revascularization (coronary artery bypass grafting; CABG) procedure. Predictors of these events were identified, and long term benefits of the prophylactic regimes, that were used to control these events, were evaluated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We selected 8642 consecutive patients, who had undergone an isolated and first time CABG procedure, between 1/3/1980 and 1/3/1995. A standard hazard function model (1) was used for statistical analysis. Efficacy of the prophylactic regimes, was examined in a group of 350 high risk patients, with a preoperative left ventricular ejection fraction 30% or less, who were recently operated since 1/1/1988. Electrophysiologic (EP) guided prophylaxis was used in 92 (26%) patients, who had survived a documented episode of SCD, and remaining 258 patients were maintained on antiarrhythmic medication on an empirical basis. A sequential EP evaluation was performed, when indicated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During an early phase of hazard, which mainly lasted for up to 3 months after CABG, incremental risk factors were preoperative LVEF 30% or less (P = 0.0007) and preoperative episodes of VT/VF (P = 0.04). This phase was followed by a constant phase with a low risk of the events, which merged into a slowly rising late phase after 6 years. EP guided prophylaxis, reduced the risk of SCD in high risk patients (P = 0.03). A sequential EP evaluation, helped to detect the problems of drug resistance and a cross over from non-sustained to sustained runs of VT/VF."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Despite a successful CABG surgery, risk of VT/VF persists. A routine EP evaluation before and after a CABG procedure is recommended in all patients with a poor left ventricular function."
        }
      ]},
